  "final_text": "The September invoice fills the missing month in your list. It changes **F2** and **F3**; **F4** stays the same.\n\n## Checking the photo\n- **Invoice EL-25-09:** billing period 01.09–30.09.2025, issued 03.10.2025.\n- **Quantity:** 41,200 kWh at 9.50 MKD/kWh = 391,400 MKD. The meter readings agree: 1,325,550 − 1,284,350 = 41,200 kWh.\n- **Supply mix:** \"not specified\", the same as on the other invoices.\n\n## Problems in the CSV\n1. **September was missing.** There was no EL-25-09 row.\n2. **October was counted twice.** EL-25-10 (38,700 kWh) appears once as the e-invoice and again as a scan booked on 2025-12-02. It's the same invoice number, so it should only count once.\n\nThese two errors nearly cancel each other out. A simple sum of the CSV gives **318.4 MWh**, which looks close enough, but it's right only by accident.\n\n## Effect on the form\n\n| Field | Before (CSV as it stands) | Corrected |\n|---|---|---|\n| **F2** Electricity use 2025 | 318.4 MWh (with the duplicate, without September) | **320.9 MWh** (measured, from 12 invoices, no duplicates) |\n| **F3** Yield / share | 180 MWh → 56.5% | **180 MWh (estimate) → ≈ 56.1% of consumption (estimate)** |\n| **F4** Payback | 8,550,000 / (180,000 × 9.50) = 5.0 years | **5.0 years (estimate)**, no change |\n\n- F2 = 279,700 kWh (11 unique invoices) + 41,200 kWh (September) = 320,900 kWh.\n- F4 doesn't change because the bank's formula uses only the yield and the price, not your consumption. It's an estimate because the 180 MWh is the installer's estimate, not a measurement.\n- At 9.50 MKD/kWh, 2025 electricity cost a total of **3,048,550 MKD**, including 391,400 MKD for September.\n\n## Notes for the file\n- **The photo is stamped \"КОПИЈА\" (copy).** Get the original e-invoice EL-25-09 from Lumivolt so the bank has a complete set of 12 e-invoices.\n- **Bookkeeping:** September doesn't appear to be booked, and the duplicate October scan should be reversed. Check this with your accountant.\n- **The solar offer** is dated 2 September 2026 and is valid for 60 days, so it expires around **1 November 2026**. That's about two weeks from 16 October. It also hasn't been signed by either side yet. If the bank's review takes longer, ask the installer to extend it.\n- **F1, F5 and F6:** none of these files contain the information for these fields, so they still need your input.",
